## Compiler Checks

After substantive Rust code changes, verify that the relevant checks pass.
DSR is the first choice for repo-level gates and release builds. Prefer RCH
only for narrow ad hoc Cargo probes or when DSR itself is unavailable.

### Batch verification — finish the work, then ONE `-p` compile

**Write all of the work first. Compile at the end.** A one-line change followed
by a full `cargo check` to "make sure the one-line change worked" is wasted
minutes: the check takes longer than the edit, and the code you write is
usually fine the first time. The mandated workflow:

1. **Do the whole task** — all edits, all files, all ideas, everything the task
   calls for. Trust the code you wrote while writing it.
2. **Then run ONE named command** — exactly one package via `-p <crate>` (or
   one named test via `-p <tests-pkg> --test <name>`). Let it run all the way
   through so it enumerates every error. Example: after editing
   `crates/emath-ir/**`, run only `cargo check -p emath-ir` (not the
   workspace, not a second crate "just in case").
3. **Fix all errors in one pass** — not one at a time.
4. **Run the same command once more** to confirm that package is clean.

**Forbidden without explicit human approval:** `cargo check` / `test` /
`build` without `-p`, `--all` / `--all-targets` / `--workspace`, or checking
sibling crates that the bead did not touch. Full-program / multi-crate
compiles exist only when the human explicitly asks to see whether the
broader tree opens — never as routine bead verification.

The compiler lists every error in a single run; collecting that full list and
fixing it in one batch is always faster than an edit-compile-see-next-error
loop, and each round trip through a remote worker costs minutes. Reserve the
exception for genuine debugging (a reproduced failure you must bisect), not for
routine authoring.

### Language (`.emath`) vs Rust nucleus — what Cargo is for

Cargo only typechecks / tests **Rust**. It does not "compile the `.emath`
language" in the abstract.

| What the bead changed | What to run |
|-----------------------|-------------|
| Only `language/reference/**`, `language/examples/**`, grammar prose, stdlib contracts, docs | **No Cargo.** Prove the bead with the docs/examples/acceptance the bead names (and any existing non-Cargo gate the bead cites). |
| Rust that **admits** that surface (parser, lexer, sema, IR, …) | **One** `-p` for the crate you edited, e.g. `cargo check -p emath-syntax`, then the bead's named `--test` if any. |
| Both docs + admitting Rust | Finish both, then the **one** Rust `-p` (and named test) for the admitting crate — still not the workspace. |

If the bead never touches a `crates/**` or `tests/**` Rust package, do not
invent a `cargo check`. Checking unrelated crates is wasted time.

