## RCH - Remote Compilation Helper

Run cargo tests and compiles for only absolutely necessary compiles.

Do not run heavy compiles or test suites for routine line edits or small textual adjustments. When you run a compile or test, you need to be testing things with vigor and for deliberate reason, not just because you changed a line. Targeted probes must have a clear diagnostic hypothesis and deliver high-signal verification.

**When RCH workers are healthy (e.g. `spark-*`), prefer `rch exec` for heavy
tests.** Batch-verify: finish the bead's edits, then **one** named
`-p <crate>` / `--test <name>` command, fix the full error list in one pass,
confirm once. A warm local `cargo check -p <crate>` is allowed as a fast
compile signal when spark is busy or excluded — still **exactly one** `-p`,
never the workspace. Local full/`--all-targets` Cargo needs explicit human
approval.

Use DSR first for repo-level gates. Use RCH for CPU-heavy Cargo probes when DSR
does not cover the needed check or when you are intentionally running a narrow
diagnostic. Default shapes (substitute the bead's package/test):

```bash
rch exec -- env CARGO_TARGET_DIR="${RCH_TARGET_BASE:-${TMPDIR:-/tmp}}/rch_target_emath_check" cargo check -p emath-ir
rch exec -- env CARGO_TARGET_DIR="${RCH_TARGET_BASE:-${TMPDIR:-/tmp}}/rch_target_emath_test" cargo test -p emath-ir-tests --test capability_reference_vm
```

**Do not** use `--all-targets` / workspace-wide Cargo unless the human
explicitly approved a full-tree open check.

**ALWAYS use `${RCH_TARGET_BASE:-${TMPDIR:-/tmp}}` as the base — never `${TMPDIR:-/tmp}`
or a bare `/tmp` — when you invent a new per-task target dir name.** 

Prefer reusing `rch_target_emath_check` / `rch_target_emath_test`. If you truly
need a distinct target dir for an isolated task, still base it on
`$RCH_TARGET_BASE` and delete it when done.

Do not rely on local fallback for heavy builds in a shared-agent environment
unless the user explicitly authorizes it. See also
`internal/prompts/COMPILE-ONE-PACKAGE.md`.

Quick diagnostics:

```bash
rch doctor
rch status
rch queue
```

